Follow-up to mlir_aie_ml_examples_npu.log, which left vision/* and the Makefile-built
ml/* designs unrun on this machine because it had neither `make` nor an OpenCV C++ dev
package. Both gaps are now filled -- this log runs those designs.

--- What was installed to unblock this ---

  - GNU Make 4.4.1 (conda-forge's latest build) into the isolated `mlir-aie-iron` conda
    env, not any of this repo's 5 tracked envs: `conda install -n mlir-aie-iron -c
    conda-forge make=4.4.1`.
  - OpenCV 5.0.0 (the current latest release; Windows prebuilt, vc16 = VS2019 ABI, binary
    compatible with the VS2022 Build Tools already on this machine per Microsoft's C++
    ABI stability guarantee) -- downloaded the official
    opencv-5.0.0-windows.exe self-extractor (195,273,354 bytes, matches the GitHub
    release asset size) and extracted with 7z to C:\Technical\thirdParty\, landing at
    C:\Technical\thirdParty\opencv\build. That exact path is mlir-aie's own CMakeLists.txt
    hardcoded default `OpenCV_DIR` for every vision/* example (see each CMakeLists.txt's
    own comment), so nothing in mlir-aie needed editing -- it just needed OpenCV to
    actually exist at the path it already assumes.
  - torch 2.14.0+cpu (latest, CPU-only wheel) into mlir-aie's own `ironenv` venv, needed
    only so two ml/* examples (bottleneck, conv2d) can generate a torch/numpy host-side
    reference to check the NPU's output against -- not used for anything performance-
    relevant, and not touching this repo's own resnet_env/resnet_env17 (CLAUDE.md's
    "don't install torch into resnet_env17" is about the pipeline's own quantize/run env;
    this is the fully separate mlir-aie-iron/ironenv toolchain).
  - A `python3.exe` shim (copy of `ironenv\Scripts\python.exe`) added to that same
    Scripts dir. mlir-aie's Makefiles shell out to a bare `python3`, which on this
    machine resolves to the Windows Store's app-execution-alias stub instead of the
    activated venv's interpreter; the shim makes `python3` resolve correctly once the
    venv's Scripts dir is first on PATH, same fix pattern as the llvm-objcopy.exe PATH
    fix in the original saxpy setup.

Two Makefile-specific quirks fixed at invocation time, not by editing mlir-aie itself:
  - `makefile-common` infers "running under WSL" from `command -v powershell.exe`
    succeeding -- true on native Windows too (powershell.exe is always on PATH), so its
    `getwslpath` variable defaults to `wslpath -w`, a WSL-only tool absent from Git Bash.
    Fixed per-invocation with a command-line override: `make getwslpath=echo ...`.
  - The vision/* CMake build finds OpenCV fine at configure time, but the built .exe
    still needs `C:\Technical\thirdParty\opencv\build\x64\vc16\bin` (the DLLs) on PATH at
    *run* time, or it fails silently under the Makefile's `powershell.exe ./foo.exe`
    wrapper. Added to PATH before invoking `make run`.
  - (Separately confirmed harmless: the Makefile's literal `-D CMAKE_C_COMPILER=cc -D
    CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=g++` flags, meant for a Linux/WSL gcc, are silently ignored by
    CMake's Visual Studio generator, which uses cl.exe from the installed VS2022 Build
    Tools toolset regardless of what's passed.)

All four `vision/*` designs are Makefile + CMake host builds: the .py generates the
xclbin/insts via mlir-aie's own JIT pipeline, then a C++ test.cpp (linking OpenCV,
built via CMake + MSBuild/VS2022) drives the actual NPU dispatch and compares against a
golden reference computed by the same test.cpp on the CPU. None given an input image, so
each generates and uses a random in-memory image of its target resolution (1920x1080).

